course: dictionary
lesson: 413
title: An appointment
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An appointment is an agreed time to meet someone, such as a doctor, dentist or hairdresser. You arrange it before you go. «The GP is the family doctor at the local surgery, and many surgeries give out the day's appointments first thing in the morning.» Giving out appointments means giving out set times, so an appointment is a time you have agreed in advance.
explain: The word can also mean choosing someone for a job, as in «the appointment of a new head teacher». Here it means the choosing itself, not the meeting. A job can also be called an appointment, especially an important one. To find the right meaning, try swapping in a simple word. If "meeting" makes sense, it is the first meaning. If "choosing" fits better, it is the second.
explain: Clues in the sentence help you choose. «Non-urgent appointments can be weeks away» tells you the meeting is far in the future. «Some appointments happen by phone or video» tells you an appointment does not always mean walking into a room. Look for words like weeks, phone, or morning, because they show you what kind of appointment is meant.
fact: An appointment is an agreed time to meet someone.
fact: «Appointment» can also mean choosing someone for a job, or the job itself.
fact: «By appointment» means only if you have arranged a time first.
fact: You can make, keep, cancel or miss an appointment.
fact: Appointments are often written in a diary or calendar so you do not forget them.
exercise: «Some appointments happen by phone or video when no examination is needed.» What is an appointment in this sentence? | A job you can apply for online | *An agreed time to meet someone, even by video | A chair at the dentist | A kind of telephone
exercise: Mum says, «I need to cancel my hair appointment.» What does cancel mean here? | Keep it and arrive early | Pay for the haircut at the door | *Stop the agreed time from going ahead | Ask the hairdresser to sing
exercise: «The appointment of a new head teacher was made in the spring.» In this sentence, what does appointment mean? | A bell that rings for lessons | A meeting at the school gate | *Choosing someone for the job | A teacher's desk
exercise: «Non-urgent appointments can be weeks away.» What does this tell you? | The appointment has already happened | The doctor has gone on holiday | The problem is very urgent | *The appointment is a long time in the future
test: A sign on a shop door says «Open by appointment only.» What does this mean? | The shop is closed for ever | *You must arrange a time first | You can walk in at any time | Only children may come in
test: Ella says, «I missed my appointment, so I need a new one.» What happened to Ella's appointment? | She arrived early and was happy | She cancelled the job | *She did not arrive at the agreed time | She chose a new doctor for the family
exam: Jayden says, «My dentist appointment is at 3:15 and it lasts 40 minutes.» When does it end? | *3:55 | 4:05 | 3:15 | 3:40
